.SH NAME
VT220 Owner's Manual, 3rd Edition (December 1984)
.
.\""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""
.SH "INTRODUCTION"
This manual provides the information you need to operate and maintain
your VT220 video terminal. The manual is organized into six chapters and
two appendices as follows.
.\" the real one has three appendices (the last one is about keyboards)
.bP
Chapter\ 1, \(lqA Look at the Terminal,\(rq introduces you to the VT220
terminal. This chapter provides an overview of what the terminal is and
what it does, then briefly describes how it works.
.bP
Chapter\ 2, \(lqControls, Indicators, and Connectors,\(rq describes the
terminal controls, indicators, and connectors, and shows their
locations.
.bP
Chapter\ 3, \(lqOperating Procedures,\(rq provides information on
specific terminal functions and operating procedures.
.bP
Chapter\ 4, \(lqTerminal Set-Up,\(rq describes each set-up screen feature
in detail. This chapter shows you how to select set-up features to
define the terminal's operating characteristics.
.bP
Chapter\ 5, \(lqCommunication,\(rq describes how the VT220 terminal
communicates with a host computer and a peripheral device such as a
printer.
.bP
Chapter\ 6, \(lqProblem Solving,\(rq describes the self-test used to
find terminal hardware problems. This chapter also contains simple
troubleshooting information to correct common operating problems.
.bP
Appendix\ A, \(lqSpecifications,\(rq provides all VT220 terminal
specifications.
.bP
Appendix\ B, \(lqOptions, Documentation, and Supplies,\(rq describes the
options, related documentation, and supplies mentioned in this manual
and how to order them.
.\" .bP
.\" Appendix C, \(lqKeyboards,\(rq shows the various keyboards available
.\" with the VT220 terminal.
.
.
.\""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""
.SH "A LOOK AT THE TERMINAL"
.SS "GENERAL"
This chapter introduces you to the VT220 terminal. The chapter provides
an overview of what the terminal is and what it does, then briefly
describes how the terminal works.
.
.SS "VT220 COMPONENTS"
The two main components of the VT220 terminal are the monitor/system
unit and the keyboard.
.\" (Figure 1-1)
.
.SSS "Monitor/System Unit"
The monitor/system unit (referred to as the \fIterminal\fR from this
point on) consists of a monochrome cathode-ray tube (CRT), a terminal
controller board, and a power supply/monitor board.
.
.SSS "Keyboard"
The low-profile keyboard has four groups of keys and four visual
indicators. The main keypad operates like a typewriter keyboard. A
single coiled cable connects the keyboard to the terminal.
.P
There are 15 basic keyboards available with the VT220.
.\" (See Appendix C.)
Each keyboard is for a different language.
.
.SS "HOW THE VT220 WORKS"
The VT220 is a general-purpose video display terminal that lets you
interact with a software application program. Characters sent by the
application program appear as text on the terminal screen. You can print
the text you create on the terminal if the terminal is connected to a
printer. The terminal operates by executing standard American National
Standards Institute (ANSI) functions.
.
.SS "OPERATING STATES"
The VT220 has three operating states you can select from the keyboard.
.bP
Set-Up
.bP
On-line
.bP
Local
.
.SSS "Set-Up"
The set-up state lets you select or examine terminal operating features.
\fITERMINAL SET-UP\fR describes these set-up features in detail. You
also use set-up to select the on-line and local states.
.P
You select set-up from the keyboard by pressing the \fBSet-Up\fR key.
.
.SSS "On-Line"
The on-line state lets the terminal communicate with a host computer.
Data entered at the keyboard is sent to the host computer. Data received
from the host computer is displayed on the monitor. You can also display
data entered from the keyboard on the screen, if you select the local
echo feature in set-up (\fI\,TERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR).
.P
You can only select on-line in set-up.
.
.SSS "Local"
The local state lets you place the host computer on hold. Data entered
at the keyboard is sent to the monitor, but not to the host computer.
Data received from the host computer is stored; this data is sent to the
monitor after you put the terminal back on-line.
.P
You can only select local in set-up.
.
.SS "OPERATING MODES"
The VT220 has four major operating modes. You can select these modes in
set-up. The default operating mode is VT220 mode, 7-bit controls. A
\fIdefault\fR setting is a factory setting the terminal uses until you
change that setting.
.bP
VT200 mode, 7-bit controls
.bP
VT200 mode, 8-bit controls
.bP
VT100 mode
.bP
VT52 mode
.
.SSS "VT200 mode, 7-Bit Controls"
This mode executes standard ANSI functions and lets you use the full
range of VT220 capabilities. You should use this mode with application
programs that expect 7-bit control characters, and either DEC
multinational characters or national replacement characters (depending
on the character set selected in set-up).
.P
.B NOTE: In general, most VT100 application programs will run in VT200
.B mode, 7-bit controls.
.
.SSS "VT200 Mode, 8-Bit Controls"
This mode also executes standard ANSI functions and lets you use the
full range of VT220 capabilities. You should use this mode with
application programs that expect 8-bit control characters and DEC
multinational characters.
.
.SSS "VT100 Mode"
This mode executes standard ANSI functions. You should use VT100 mode
with application programs that require strict compatibility with
Digital's VT100 terminal.
.
.SSS "VT52 Mode"
This is a text mode that executes Digital (DEC) private functions (not
ANSI). You should use VT52 mode for compatibility with existing
application programs designed for Digital's VT52 terminal.
.
.SS "CHARACTER SET MODES"
The VT220 has two basic character set modes, multinational and national.
.P
.I Multinational
mode supports the DEC multinational character set (DEC MCS). The DEC MCS
is an 8-bit character set that contains most characters used in the
major European languages. The ASCII character set is included in the DEC
MCS.
.P
.I National
mode supports the national replacement character (NRC) sets. The NRC
sets are a group of eleven 7-bit character sets. You can only use one
NRC set at a time. The NRC set available depends on the keyboard
selected in set-up (\fI\,TERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR). National mode restricts
compatibility to a 7-bit environment, so the DEC MCS is not available.
.
.SS "CRT SAVER FEATURE"
If during normal operation the terminal is inactive for 30 minutes (no
keyboard activity or input from a host computer), the monitor screen
goes blank (but data is not lost). Keyboard activity or input from the
host computer activates the monitor again. To reactivate the screen,
press the \fBCtrl\fR key.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B This manual describes the use of the North American keyboard, unless
.B otherwise specified. When you use the North American keyboard, the
.B terminal defaults to multinational mode. (National mode is disabled).
.
.
.\""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""
.SH "CONTROLS, INDICATORS, AND CONNECTORS"
.SS "GENERAL"
This chapter provides information about the terminal's controls,
indicators, and connectors. The chapter also describes the keyboard,
including the three keypads and special function keys.
.
.SS "TERMINAL"
The terminal controls, indicators, and connectors are
.\" shown in Figures 2-1 and 2-2 and
described in Tables 2-1 and 2-2.
.
.SSS "Monitor"
The monitor controls are
.\" shown in Figure 2-2 and
described in Table 2-2.
.
.TBL "2-1" "Terminal Controls, Indicators, and Connctors"
.TS
lB lBX
lT lTX.
Control/indicator/Connector	Description

Power switch	Turns the terminal on or off.
	Pressing \fB1\fR turns power on.
	Pressing \fB0\fR turns power off.

EIA host port connector	T{
Connects the terminal to a host computer either directly or via a modem.
T}

20 mA host port connector	T{
Connects the terminal to a nearby host computer via a 20 mA connection.
T}

T{
Composite video output connector
T}	T{
Provides a complete video output signal to an additional slave monitor.
T}

Power OK indicator	T{
Turns on to indicate power is applied to the terminal.
T}

Printer port connector	T{
Connects a printer to the terminal.
T}

Keyboard connector	T{
Connects the keyboard cable to the terminal.
T}

AC input connector	T{
Connects the power cord to the terminal from the wall outlet.
T}

Fuse	T{
Protects the system from electrical damage.
T}

Voltage select switch	T{
This slide switch lets you match the terminal voltage to the wall outlet
voltage. See the \fIVT220 Installation Guide\fR for the correct setting.
T}

.T&
l sX.
\fBCAUTION: An incorrect voltage setting can damage the terminal.\fR

.T&
l lTX.
Cursor	T{
Indicates where the next display character will appear on the monitor
screen. You can select an underline cursor or block cursor in the
Display Set-Up screen (\fI\,TERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR).
T}
.TE
.
.TBL "2-2" "Monitor Controls and Tilt Leg Relase Button"
.TS
lB lBX
lT lTX.
Control	Description

Contrast control	T{
Adjusts the degree of contrast on the monitor screen.
T}

Brightness	T{
Adjusts the degree of brightness on the monitor screen.
T}

Tilt leg release button	T{
Adjusts the viewing angle of the monitor. Pressing the button releases a
tilt leg that drops to provide a -5 to +15 degree tilt range.
T}
.TE
.
.SS "KEYBOARD"
The keyboard
.\" (Figure 2-3)
consists of the following parts.
.P
.RS
Main keypad
.br
Editing keypad
.br
Auxiliary keypad
.br
Top-row function keys
.br
Four visual indicators
.br
Two audible indicators
.RE
.
.SS "MAIN KEYPAD"
This keypad
.\" (Figure 2-4)
operates like a standard typewriter keyboard.
.P
The main keypad ahs the following special function keys.
.
.SSS "Tab"
Pressing the \fBTab\fR key sends a horizontal tab, which normally moves
the cursor to the next tab stop.
.
.SSS "Ctrl"
Holding down the \fBCtrl\fR key and pressing another key sends a control
code to the system. A control code tells the system to perform a
predefined operation.
.P
In this manual, keyboard control functions using \fBCtrl\fR appear as
follows.
.P
.RS
.B Ctrl-(other key)
.RE
.P
For example, \fBCtrl-Z\fR means to press and hold \fBCtrl\fR while
pressing the \fBZ\fR key.
.
.SSS "Lock"
Pressing the \fBLock\fR key down makes the alphabetic keys send
uppercase characters. Pressing \fBLock\fR again makes the alphabetic
keys send lowercase characters. \fBLock\fR is similar to the Shift Lock
key on a typewriter.
.
.SSS "Shift"
Holding down the \fBShift\fR key and pressing another key sends
uppercase characters, or the top symbol on two-character keys.
.P
In some cases, you use \fBShift\fR with another key to send a predefined
control function. In this manual, a keyboard control function using
\fBShift\fR is shown as follows.
.P
.RS
.B Shift-(other key)
.RE
.P
For example, \fBShift-Print Screen\fR means to press and hold
\fBShift\fR while pressing the \fBPrint Screen\fR key.
.
.SSS "Return"
Pressing the \fBReturn\fR key sends either a carriage return or a
carriage return and line feed (selected in the General Set-Up screen,
\fITERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR). In some cases, \fBReturn\fR moves the cursor
to the next line when editing text. In others, \fBReturn\fR is a signal
to the system that a particular operation is complete.
.
.SSS "<X (Delete)"
Pressing the \fB<X\fR (delete) key sends a DEL (delete) character.
Normally \fB<X\fR (delete) erases one character to the left of the
cursor. Typing \fBShift-<X\fR (delete) sends a CAN (cancel) character.
.
.SSS "Compose Character"
This key lets you create characters that do not exist as standard keys
on your keyboard. See the \(lqComposing Characters\(rq section in
\fIOPERATING PROCEDURES\fR to use this key.
.
.SS "EDITING KEYPAD"
Normally, you use the editing keypad
.\" (Figure 2-5)
to control the cursor and edit data that you already entered.
.P
In a typical editing operation, the four arrow keys move the cursor in
the direction indicated by the arrow. The six editing keys have
functions corresponding to their legends. See your application software
manual for specific information.
.
.SS "AUXILIARY KEYPAD"
The auxiliary keypad
.\" (Figure 2-6)
lets you enter numeric data as you would with a standard calculator.
Some keys
.RB ( PF1 ,
.BR PF2 ,
.BR PF3 ,
and
.RB PF4 )
may have functions assigned by the application software. See your
application software manual for specific information.
.P
The \fBEnter\fR key can cause a carriage return or a carriage return and
line feed, depending on the General Set-Up screen selection. You also
use \fBEnter\fR in set-up, to activate a selected feature.
.
.SS "TOP-ROW FUNCTION KEYS"
Most of the top-row function keys
.\" (Figure 2-7)
have functions assigned by the application software. Your application
software manual should describe the function of these keys. The
following paragraphs describe the predefined top-row keys.
.
.SSS "Hold Screen"
Pressing the \fBHold Screen\fR key freezes the screen display and stops
any new characters from being displayed. The Hold Screen indicator comes
on when you press this key. Pressing \fBHold Screen\fR again returns the
terminal to normal operation and turns off the Hold Screen indicator.
.
.SSS "Print Screen"
Pressing the \fBPrint Screen\fR key sends the text on the screen to the
printer.
.P
Typing \fBCtrl-Print Screen\fR sets or resets auto print mode. See the
\(lqAuto Print Mode\(rq section in \fIOPERATING PROCEDURES\fB.
.
.SSS "Set-Up"
Pressing the \fBSet-Up\fR key causes the terminal to enter or exit the
set-up state. (See \fITERMINAL SET-UP\fR.)
.
.SSS "Data/Talk"
The \fBData/Talk\fR key only operates if EIA modem controls are enabled.
(See \fIOPERATING PROCEDURES\fR.)
.
.SSS "Break"
The \fBBreak\fR key works alone or with other keys to perform an
operation.
.bP
Pressing \fBBreak\fR alone sends a break if break is enabled in set-up.
(See the \(lqKeyboard Set-Up Screen\(rq section in \fITERMINAL
SET-UP\fR.)
.bP
Typing \fBShift-Break\fR starts a disconnect. (See the
\(lqConnect/Disconnect\(rq section in \fICOMMUNICATION\fR.)
.bP
Typing \fBCtrl-Break\fR sends the answerback message to the host
computer. (See the \(lqKeyboard Set-Up Screen\(rq in \fITERMINAL
SET-UP\fR.)
.
.SSS "F11 (ESC)"
\fBF11\fR is normally a function key used by application programs. In
VT100 and VT52 mode, it sends an ESC (escape) character.
.
.SSS "F12 (BS)"
\fBF12\fR is normally a function key used by application programs. In
VT100 and VT52 modes, it sends a BS (backspace) character.
.
.SSS "F13 (LF)"
\fBF13\fR is normally a function key used by application programs. In
VT100 and VT52 modes, it sends an LF (line feed) character.
.
.SSS "Function Key Summary"
The following keys are function keys used by application programs. Each
key takes on a meaning defined by the particular application program.
The meaning of a key may or may not correspond to the legend on the key.
The following list shows typical uses for each key. Actual use depends
on the application.
.P
.TS
lB lB
l l.
Application-Defined Keys	Cursor Positioning Keys

F6	(\(<-)
F7	(\(->)
F8	(\(ua)
F9	(\(da)
F10
F11 (ESC)	\fBEditing Keys\fR
F12 (BS)
F13 (LF)	Find
F14	Insert Here
Help	Remove
Do	Select
F17	Prev Screen
F18	Next Screen
F19
F20

PF1
PF2
PF3
PF4
.TE
.
.SS "VISUAL INDICATORS"
The keyboard has four visual indicators showing the current terminal
status or operation in progress.
.
.SSS "Hold Screen Indicator"
The Hold Screen indicator is on when the video monitor screen is frozen.
See the \(lqHold Screen\(rq key description.
.
.SSS "Lock Indicator"
The Lock indicator comes on to indicate that the terminal will send
uppercase characters only. See the \(lqLock\(rq key description.
.
.SSS "Compose Indicator"
The Compose indicator comes on to indicate you are performing a compose
sequence. (See the \(lqComposing Characters\(rq section in \fIOPERATING
PROCEDURES\fR.)
.
.SSS "Wait Indicator"
The Wait indicator is on when the terminal is prevented (locked) from
sending information. You can clear this locked condition by invoking the
\fBClear Comm\fR feature from the Set-Up Directory screen (\fI\,TERMINAL
SET-UP\/\fR.) See \fICOMMUNICATION\fR for the causes of the keyboard
lock condition.
.
.SS "AUDIBLE INDICATORS"
The keyboard can generate two sounds you select from the Keyboard Set-Up
screen (\fI\,TERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR), a keyclick and a bell. You can use
the bell as a margin bell, warning bell, or both.
.
.SSS "Keyclick"
The keyclick sound occurs each time you press a key, with the following
exceptions.
.bP
You press \fBShift\fR or \fBCtrl\fR. These keys do not make a keyclick,
because they do not send a character. They modify characters sent by
other keys.
.bP
When the Wait indicator is on; characters from the keyboard are lost.
.bP
The keyclick set-up feature is off.
.
.SSS "Bell"
The bell tone sounds in each of the following cases.
.bP
As part of the power-up self-test
.bP
When the terminal receives a bell (BEL) character from the computer
.bP
After a compose character error
.bP
When the cursor approaches the right margin
.
.
.\""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""
.SH "OPERATING PROCEDURES"
.SS GENERAL
This chapter helps you to become familiar with some specific terminal
functions and operating procedures. However, your main reference source
is the documentation for your application software.
.
.SS PRINTING
The VT220 has a built-in serial printer interface that supports the
following optional Digital printers.
.P
.RS
LA34/38
.br
LA35/LA36
.br
LA12
.br
LA100
.br
LA120
.br
LA50
.br
LQP02
.RE
.P
The VT220 printing functions operate in one of four modes selected from
set-up screens (\fI\,TERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR).
.P
.TS
lB lB
l l.
Mode	Set-Up Screen

Normal (default)	Printer Set-Up

Auto print	Printer Set-Up

Printer controller	Printer Set-Up

Local controller	Set-Up Directory and
	Printer Set-Up
.TE
.P
These modes let the terminal perform several print operations selected
from the keyboard and/or the computer.
.
.SSS "Normal Mode"
Normal mode lets you select all local printing functions (such as
\fBPrint Full Page\fR) from the keyboard.
.
.SSS "Auto Print Mode"
Auto print mode prints the current display line when the cursor moves to
the next line. The cursor moves to the next line when the terminal (1)
receives a line feed, form feed, or vertical tab code, or (2)
automatically wraps the line. When selected, \fBAuto Print Mode\fR
appears on the status line in set-up. You can use all keyboard printing
functions (such as \fBPrint Full Page\fR) in auto print mode.
.P
To invoke auto print mode, type \fBCtrl-Print Screen\fR. To exit auto
print mode, type \fBCtrl-Print Screen\fR again.
.
.SSS "Printer Controller Mode"
In printer controller mode, the host computer has direct control of the
printer. Characters received from the host computer go directly to the
printer, and are not displayed on the screen. (See
\fICOMMUNICATIONS\fR.) When selected, \fBPrinter Controller Mode\fR
appears on the status line in set-up. You cannot select this mode from
the keyboard (except by entering set-up).
.P
You cannot use local printing functions in printer controller mode. For
example, \fBPrint Full Page\fR does not work.
.
.SSS "Local Controller Mode"
Local controller mode is a special mode that lets you send information
directly from the keyboard to the printer. You may find this feature
useful in setting up certain printer for operation, without involving
the host computer.
.P
To select local controller mode, you must select two different set-up
features.
.nP 1
\fBLocal\fR \(em selected in the Set-Up Directory screen.
.nP 2
\fBPrinter Controller Mode\fR \(em selected in the Printer Set-Up
screen.
.P
When you select these two set-up features, the terminal is in local
controller mode.
.
.SS "OPTIONAL MODEM"
An optional modem lets the VT220 communicate over a telephone line with
a remote host computer. The VT220 accepts compatible modems such as the
AT&T 103, 113, and 212 types, in addition to Digital's DF02 and DF03.
(See \fIOPTIONS, DOCUMENTATION, AND SUPPLIES\fR.) See
\fICOMMUNICATION\fR for more information on communications.
.
.SS "COMPOSING CHARACTERS"
You can use \fIcompose sequences\fR to create characters that do not
exist as standard keys on your keyboard. To use a compose sequence, you
press a series of keys. There are two types of compose sequences:
three-stroke sequences and two-stroke sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B The compose sequences you can use depend on the character set mode
.B selected (multinational or national). Table 3-1 lists the valid
.B compose sequences for multinational mode. Table 3-2 lists the valid
.B compose sequences for each keyboard in national mode. In national
.B mode, the compose sequences you can use depend on the keyboard
.B selected.
.P
You can use three-stroke sequences on all VT220 keyboards. First you
press the \fBCompose Character\fR key, then you press two standard keys
whose characters form a valid compose sequence.
.P
You can use two-stroke sequences on all keyboards except the North
American keyboard. Two-stroke sequences are faster than three-stroke
sequences, but are limited to sequences starting with the following
nonspacing diacritical marks: grave accent, acute accent, circumflex
accent, tilde mark, diaeresis mark (umlaut), and ring mark. Two-stroke
sequences do not use the \fBCompose Character\fR key. Instead, you enter
a nonspacing diacritical mark first. Then enter a standard character
that forms a valid compose sequence with the diacritical mark.
.P
Diacritical marks are available on all but the North American keyboard.
The diacritical marks vary among the keyboards, depending on relative
use. Also, some keyboards have keys that contain both a standard
character and a diacritical mark.
.P
As with standard keys, you select the character you want with the
\fBShift\fR and \fBLock\fR keys.
.P
If you use a diacritical mark with a three-stroke sequence, the
diacritical mark is treated as its equivalent character.
.P
.RS
.TS
lB lB
l l.
Diacritical Mark	Equivalent Character

Diaeresis (umlaut) mark	Double quote \(dq
Acute accent	Apostrophe \(aq
Grave accent	Single quote \(ga
Circumflex accent	Circumflex character \(ha
Tilde mark	Tilde character \(ti
Ring mark	Asterisk * or degree \(de
.TE
.RE
.P
Tables 3-1 and 3-2 list all valid compose sequences for multinational
and national modes respectively. Depending on the keyboard, you can
create characters in column 1 in one or more ways.
.P
.bP
With a standard key (if available on that keyboard)
.bP
With a three-stroke compose sequence (always)
.bP
With a two-stroke compose sequence (if the diacritical mark is available
on the keyboard)
.
.SSS "Using a Three-Stroke Compose Sequence"
Create a three-stroke compose sequence as follows.
.nP 1
Find the character you want to create in column 1 of Table 3-1 or 3-2.
.nP 2
Press the \fBCompose Character\fR key. (The Compose indicator comes on,
indicating the terminal is in compose mode.)
.nP 3
Type the two characters in column 2 for the character you want to
create.
.P
For example, to create e with acute accent, press \fBCompose
Character\fR, and then type e and apostrophe; or press \fBCompose
Character\fR, and then type apostrophe and e.
.P
When you complete a valid sequence, the Compose indicator turns off and
the composite character is sent to the application. If you use an
invalid sequence, the sequence is aborted and the bell sounds (if the
warning bell is enabled in the Keyboard Set-Up screen, \fITERMINAL
SET-UP\/\fR).
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Function keys abort a compose sequence without sounding the bell.
.
.SSS "Using a Two-Stroke Compose Sequence"
Create a two-stroke compose sequence as follows.
.\" in the original manual, the "a" is missing
.P
.B NOTE:
.B You can use two-stroke compose sequences on all keyboards except the
.B North American keyboard.
.
.nP 1
Find the character you want to create in column 1 of Table 3-1 or 3-2.
Verify in column 3 that the character can be created.
.nP 2
Press the key with the diacritical mark shown in column 3. (The Compose
indicator comes on, indicating the terminal is in compose mode.)
.nP 3
Type the second character shown in column 3.
.P
For example, to create e with grave accent on a Danish keyboard, press
the key that has the grave accent and then type e.
.P
When you complete a valid sequence, the Compose indicator turns off and
the Composite character is sent to the application. If you use an
invalid sequence, the sequence is aborted and the bell sounds (if the
warning bell is enabled in the Keyboard Set-Up screen, \fITERMINAL
SET-UP\/\fR).
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Function keys abort a compose sequence without sounding the bell.
.
.SSS "Aborting or Restarting a Compose Sequence"
If you accidentally enter compose mode (by pressing the \fBCompose
Character\fR key or a diacritical mark key), press the \fB<X\fR (delete)
key to immediately terminate the compose sequence and exit compose mode.
No character is sent to the application.
.P
If you press \fBCompose Character\fR during a compose sequence, a new
three-stroke sequence starts from that point. The previous sequence is
aborted, with no effect on the application.
.
