.SSS "Fields"
The fields on each screen are blocks of text describing current
operating characteristics. There are three types of fields.
.
.\" technically the headings should be .I but they are changed to .B
.\" here since this is what other headings use too
.nP 1
.B Action Field
.IP
An action field has only one value. When you select an action field and
press the \fBEnter\fR key, the terminal performs the action.
.IP
For example, each screen has an action field that reads \fBTo
Directory\fR. When you select this field and press \fBEnter\fR, the
Set-Up Directory screen replaces the current screen.
.nP 2
.B Parameter Field
.IP
A parameter field contains a feature that has two or more values. When
you select a parameter field and press \fBEnter\fR, the next value
replaces the current value.
.IP
For example, if you select the keyclick parameter field, it may have a
value of \fBKeyclick\fR. Pressing \fBEnter\fR changes the field to \fBNo
Keyclick\fR.
.nP 3
.B Text Parameter Field
.IP
A text parameter field lets you enter a value from the keyboard. You can
select a text parameter field as follows.
.IP
.RS
.nP 1
Use the arrow keys to move the field cursor to the next parameter field.
(See the following \(lqSet-Up Controls and Cursor\(rq section.)
.nP 2
Press \fBEnter\fR. The terminal prompts you to enter text on the status
line at the bottom of the screen, temporarily overwriting the status
line.
.nP 3
Type the text you want entered as the new value. The value appears next
to the prompt.
.nP 4
Press \fBEnter\fR to enter the new value.
.RE
.IP
If you make a mistake, press the \fB<X\fR (delete) key to erase the last
character entered. If you want to abort the entry without changing the
original value, press an arrow key to change the field selection. (See
the following \(lqSet-Up Controls and Cursor\(rq section.)
.
.SSS "Set-Up Controls and Cursor"
The VT220 uses a field cursor while in set-up. The field cursor appears
as a highlighted field (reverse video) that you can move from field to
field with the arrow keys.
.P
Table 4-2 describes the keys used to enter and exit set-up, move the
field cursor, and change operating characteristics.
.
.TBL "4-2" "Set-Up Controls and Cursor Functions"
.TS
lB lBX
lT lTX.
Control Key	Function

Set-Up	T{
Pressing the \fBSet-Up\fR key one time places the terminal in set-up.
Pressing \fBSet-Up\fR again returns the terminal to the operating state
(on-line or local).
T}

Arrow keys	T{
Pressing the arrow keys moves the field cursor in the direction of the
arrow.
T}

Enter	T{
The \fBEnter\fR key lets you perform the function displayed at the field
cursor position.

If the cursor is on an action field, pressing \fBEnter\fR immediately
performs the action.

If the cursor is on a parameter field, pressing \fBEnter\fR changes the
value of the field. You can use the \fBEnter\fR key to see the range of
available field values. The value displayed is the current value
invoked.
T}
.TE
.
.SS "SET-UP EXAMPLE: CHANGING FEATURES"
This section provides an example of changing the terminal operating
characteristics in set-up.
.P
Suppose the terminal is currently set to display its text in 80 columns,
and the keyboard keys click each time you press them.
.P
You decide to change these two operating characteristics so the terminal
displays 132 columns, and the keys do not click when pressed.
.P
Use the following procedure to change these two operating
characteristics in set-up.
.P
.nP 1
Press the \fBSet-Up\fR key. The terminal enters set-up and displays the
Set-Up Directory screen.
.\" (Figure 4-3)
.nP 2
Note that the cursor is on the \fBDisplay\fR field.
.nP 3
Press the \fBEnter\fR key. The terminal replaces the Set-Up Directory
screen with the Display Set-Up screen.
.\" (Figure 4-4)
.nP 4
Use the arrow keys to move the field cursor to the \fB80 Columns\fR
field.
.nP 5
Press \fBEnter\fR. The field changes from \fB80 Columns\fR to \fB132
Columns\fR, indicating that the feature changed.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Although many parameter changes are immediate (such as the column
.B feature), some changes do not take effect until you exit set-up.
.nP 6
Use the arrow keys to move the field cursor to the \fBTo Directory\fR
field. (You want to change another feature.)
.nP 7
Press \fBEnter\fR. The terminal replaces the Display Set-Up screen with
the Set-Up Directory screen.
.nP 8
Use the arrow keys to move the field cursor to the \fBKeyboard\fR field.
.nP 9
Press \fBEnter\fR. The terminal replaces the Set-Up Directory screen
with the Keyboard Set-Up screen.
.\" (Figure 4-8)
.nP 10
Use the arrow keys to move the field cursor to the \fBKeyclick\fR field.
.nP 11
Press \fBEnter\fR. The field changes from \fBKeyclick\fR to \fBNo
Keyclick\fR, indicating that the feature changed.
.nP 12
Press \fBSet-Up\fR to exit set-up and return to the operating mode
(on-line or local).
.
