.SS "TERMINAL \(em HOST DATA FLOW CONTROL"
The terminal stores incoming characters in a character input buffer and
processes the characters on a first-in/first-out basis. The size of the
input buffer is 254 characters. When the input buffer fills to 64 or 128
characters (selected from the Communications Set-Up screen), the
terminal sends an XOFF character (if you enabled XOFF from the
Communications Set-Up screen) to stop the host computer from sending
more characters. If the computer fails to respond to the XOFF character,
the terminal sends a second XOFF character when the input buffer fills
to 220 characters. The terminal sends a third XOFF character when the
buffer is full.
.P
When the input buffer contents falls below 32 characters, the terminal
sends an XON character to tell the host computer to start sending
characters again.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B If you disable XOFF in set-up, the terminal does not send XOFF to the
.B host computer when the input buffer fills. The Hold Screen key is
.B also disabled. With XOFF disabled, there is no way to ensure that
.B data will not be lost.
.P
If you enable XON/XOFF, the terminal recognizes received XON and XOFF
characters. When the terminal receives XOFF, it stops sending data
(except XON/XOFF characters). If the keyboard data buffer overflows, the
keyboard locks and the Wait indicator turns on. The terminal resumes
transmission when it receives an XON.
.
.SSS "Conditions That Send XON"
When you enable the XOFF/XON feature in set-up, the following conditions
send XON.
.bP
The number of characters in the input buffer reaches the XON point (32
characters) and the last flow control character sent was XOFF.
.bP
A clear comm operation is performed.
.bP
A recall operation is performed.
.bP
The power self-test is completed (\fI\,PROBLEM SOLVING\/\fR).
.bP
You press the \fBHold Screen\fR key to release the screen when the input
buffer is at or below the XON point.
.
.SSS "Conditions That Send XOFF"
When you enable the XOFF/XON feature in set-up, the following conditions
send XOFF.
.bP
The number of characters in the input buffer reaches the first XOFF
point (64 or 128 characters, selected from the Communications Set-Up
screen) for the first time since the last XON was sent.
.bP
The number of characters in the input buffer reaches the second XOFF
point (220 characters) for the first time since the last XON was sent.
.bP
The terminal receives a character when the input buffer is full (256
characters).
.
.SSS "Buffer Overflow Prevention"
If the host computer does not respond to the XOFF from the terminal, the
input buffer continues to fill with characters. If the buffer is filled
and characters are still coming, the buffer overflows and characters are
lost. In place of lost characters, the terminal displays reverse
question mark characters (\fB\*[error]\fR).
.P
You can use the following formulas to determine how fast the host
computer must respond to the first XOFF character, to prevent loss of
characters due to buffer overflow. Calculate the overflow first, then
host response time.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B These formulas assume that you set the transmit rate limit feature in
.B the Communications Set-Up screen to \f(BIUnlimited\fR.
.
.nP 1
.I Overflow
.IP
OVFL = (MXBF - XOFF) - [3 \(mu (RCDR/XMDR)] - (RCDR/600)
.P
where:
.nI
OVFL = the number of characters to overflow
.IP
MXBF = the receive buffer size (254 characters)
.IP
XOFF = the first XOFF point (64 or 128)
.IP
RCDR = the received data rate (receive speed)
.IP
XMDR = the transmitted data rate (transmit speed)
.nP 2
.I Host Response Time
.IP
HRST = OVFL \(mu [(DATA + STOP + PRTY + 1)/RCDR]
.P
where:
.nI
HRST = the host computer response time (in seconds)
.IP
OVFL = the number of characters to overflow
.IP
DATA = the number of data bits per character
.IP
STOP = the number of stop bits per character
.IP
PRTY = the number of parity bits per character
.P
.I Example
.br
The VT220 sends and receives 8-bit characters with no parity at 4800
bits per second. There is 1 stop bit. XOFF is sent when the buffer has
64 characters in it.
.P
.RS
.TS
n.
OVFL \&= (254 - 64) - [3 \(mu 4800/4800)] - (4800/600)
\&= 179 characters
.TE
.P
.TS
n.
HRST \&= 179 \(mu [(8 bits + 1 bit + 0 bits + 1)/4800]
\&= 0.37 seconds
.TE
.RE
.P
Therefore, the host computer must stop sending data in 0.37 seconds, or
the terminal input buffer will overflow.
.
.SSS "Use of Fill Characters"
Software that does not support XON/XOFF characters from the terminal can
still use all terminal features by using fill characters. In some
applications, you can use the terminal without XON/XOFF support or fill
characters. However, the bit rate must be limited to 9600. And the
software must not send the ESC (escape) code, or use slow scrolling,
split screen, or the printer port.
.
.SSS "Connect/Disconnect"
When a connection is made to the host computer via a modem, the terminal
performs the following operations to ensure it is ready to send and
receive.
.bP
Unlocks the keyboard (if it was locked).
.bP
Clears any transmit in progress.
.bP
Clears the keyboard buffer and all message buffers.
.bP
Clears the input buffer.
.bP
Clears XOFF sent and XOFF received.
.P
The following conditions cause a communications line disconnect.
.P
.bP
Typing \fBShift-Break\fR
.bP
Invoking \fBRecall\fR or \fBDefault\fR values in the Set-Up Directory
screen
.bP
Loss of DSR
.bP
Loss of RLSD for a time you defined in set-up
.bP
No RLSD within 30 seconds after DSR
.bP
A self-test command received from the host computer
.bP
Switching from the EIA port to the 20 mA port, or from the 20 mA port to
the EIA port"
.P
The usual way to disconnect the terminal from the communications line at
the end of communications is to type \fBShift-Break\fR. The host
computer's response to the disconnect signal depends on the computer and
the software.
.
.SS "TERMINAL-PRINTER DATA FLOW CONTROL"
The VT220 sends only data characters to the printer; the terminal does
not send XON/XOFF. The terminal recognizes only XON/XOFF from the
printer (any other characters from the printer are ignored).
.P
When the terminal receives XOFF from the printer, the terminal stops
sending data. The terminal starts sending data again when it receives an
XON, or when a clear comm operation is performed.
.
.SSS "Note on Printer Installation"
Using an 8-bit setting for the printer port line implies the use of
8-bit C1 control characters. Using a 7-bit setting implies the use of
the 7-bit ESC [ form of C1 control characters.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Older printers may not recognize the 8-bit form of C1 control
.B characters. With these printers, you must set the printer line to
.B 7-bits for correct operation.
.
.
