.SH "PROBLEM SOLVING"
.SS GENERAL
This chapter describes what to do if you have a problem with the VT220.
The chapter provides a problem checklist and describes the power-up
self-test.
.
.SS "COMMON OPERATING PROBLEMS"
Table 6-1 lists common operating problems and their possible solutions.
Check this list before calling for service.
.
.TBL "6-1" "Common Operating Problems"
.TS
lB lBX
lT lTX.
Problem	Possible Solution

T{
The terminal does not power up when the power switch is set to \fB1\fR
(on).
T}	T{
Make sure the terminal power cord is plugged into the wall outlet. Check
for power at the wall outlet by plugging in a lap to see if it lights.

Make sure the voltage selection is in the correct position. (See the
\fIVT220 Installation Guide\fR for the correct setting.)

Check the fuse and replace if necessary. (If the fuse blows again, there
is a possible shorting problem. Contact Digital Field Service.)
T}

T{
The printer does not print.
T}	T{
Make sure the printer is plugged in and its power switch is on.

Make sure the cable connection between the printer and terminal is
tight.

Make sure all communication features on the terminal and printer (such
as baud rate and parity) match.
T}

T{
The monitor display does not resume scrolling. The Hold Screen indicator
is on.
T}	T{
Press the \fBHold Screen\fR key to resume scrolling.
T}

T{
The terminal seems to be locked and does not respond to data sent from
the host.
T}	T{
Clear the terminal by using the \fBClear Comm\fR field in the Set-Up
Directory Screen (\fI\,TERMINAL SET-UP\/\fR).
T}

T{
The screen is blank, and the power OK indicator is on.
T}	T{
The CRT saver feature (\fI\,A LOOK AT THE TERMINAL\/\fR) may be on.
Press any key to reactivate the screen display.

Make sure the brightness and contrast controls are correctly adjusted.
T}

T{
The bell tone does not sound when the terminal is turned on. Keyboard
visual indicators are not on.
T}	T{
Make sure the keyboard is connected to the terminal.
T}
.TE
.
.SS "POWER-UP SELF-TEST"
The power-up self-test runs automatically each time you power up the
terminal. During the test cycle, the power-up self-test has full control
of the terminal. The terminal cannot respond to commands other than
those used for the test itself. When the test cycle ends, control
returns to the terminal.
.P
In the self-test mode, the monitor screen and the keyboard indicators
(Hold Screen, Lock, Compose, and Wait) provide information about the
terminal operating status. The monitor screen displays a text message,
and the keyboard indicators provide a coded message.
.
.SS "PERFORMING THE POWER-UP SELF-TEST"
Start the power-up self-test by setting the terminal power switch to
\fB1\fR (on).
.P
During the self-test the following events should occur.
.bP
All keyboard indicators turn on and off.
.bP
The bell tone sounds.
.P
A successful power-up self-test ends with all keyboard indicators off
and the screen displaying the message \(lqVT220 OK\(rq.
.\" original manual: [...] message shown in Figure 6-1.
The message is erased when a character is received from the host
computer, or when you press any key.
.P
The terminal displays an error message on the screen (if possible) if
the test finds any error. Table 6-2 explains the error messages
displayed on the screen.
.
.TBL "6-2" "Screen Error Messages"
.TS
lB lBX
lBT lTX.
Error Message	Problem

VT220 NVR Error \- 1	T{
.I Terminal controller board.
The nonvolatile memory (set-up storage) is not operating.
T}

VT220 EIA Port Data	T{
.I Terminal controller board.
The EIA host port is not operating.
T}
Error \- 2	\^

VT220 Keyboard	T{
.I Keyboard.
The terminal can only receive input from the host computer.
T}
Error \- 4	\^

VT220 Printer Port	T{
.I Printer port.
The terminal is operating, but cannot perform printing functions.
T}
Error \- 6	\^
.TE
.
.SS "DIGITAL SERVICE"
If the self-test indicates a problem, call your local Digital Field
Service number for assistance. Before calling, make sure to note the
exact nature of the problem, when it occurred, and any error messages or
codes that appeared.
.P
Information about your warranty, Digital services, terminal supplies,
and spare parts is provided on the inside of the back cover of this
manual.
.
.
